{{!-- Faith Received — Ask panel markup, shared between the standalone
     /the-faith-received/ask/ route (custom-faith-ask.hbs) and the "Ask"
     tab on /the-faith-received/search/ (custom-faith-search.hbs,
     [data-fs-panel="ask"]). assets/js/page/faith-ask.js binds to this
     markup with plain, unscoped document.querySelector() calls (see that
     file's own header) — it doesn't care which page it's on, only that
     the markup exists exactly once on whichever page loads it, which is
     true here since the two routes never render at the same time. This
     partial exists so there is exactly one copy of that markup to keep in
     sync with the JS, instead of two.

     Whichever page includes this partial must also load, at the bottom
     of its own template and in this relative order, feature-gate.js
     (gates the submit button below to paid members) then
     page/faith-ask.js (the DOM binding) — see custom-faith-ask.hbs's own
     script block for the working order; page-template scripts run before
     site.min.js, so both must be loaded this way rather than assumed
     from the boot bundle.

     DOM contract faith-ask.js depends on — re-read that file before
     touching any of this:
       - form[data-ask-form] > textarea[data-ask-input],
         button[data-ask-submit] — the question box.
       - [data-ask-usage] wraps two rows. faith-ask.js finds the second
         (global-capacity) row via `usageMineRow.nextElementSibling`, NOT
         a data attribute — the [data-ask-usage-mine] row and the
         unlabeled row right after it must stay exactly adjacent siblings
         in that order.
       - [data-ask-status], [data-ask-result] containing
         [data-ask-heading], [data-ask-answer], [data-ask-gaps], and
         [data-ask-error] as siblings of [data-ask-result].
       - [data-ask-footnotes-wrap] > [data-ask-footnotes], immediately
         followed by [data-ask-sources-empty] — the CSS rule
         ".ask-footnotes-wrap:not([hidden]) ~ .ask-sources-empty" in
         faith-received.css depends on that sibling order to hide the
         empty-state line once a real answer has citations.
       - button[data-feature-gate="ask"] on the submit button — the
         paid-member gate (assets/js/feature-gate.js intercepts the
         click). Do not remove or weaken this: any LLM-calling feature on
         this site is a standing member-gate + rate-limit requirement,
         and the worker itself enforces the same gate server-side (see
         faith-ask.js's header comment). --}}
<div class="ask-layout">
  <div class="ask-main">

    <form class="ask-form" data-ask-form>
      <label class="ask-label" for="ask-question">Your question</label>
      <textarea
        id="ask-question"
        class="ask-input"
        data-ask-input
        rows="3"
        maxlength="600"
        placeholder="e.g. What does the tradition teach about justification?"
        required
      ></textarea>
      <div class="ask-form-footer">
        <p class="ask-form-hint">Enter to ask &middot; Shift+Enter for a new line</p>
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary ask-submit" data-feature-gate="ask" data-ask-submit>
          Ask the library
        </button>
      </div>
    </form>

    {{!-- Usage meter -- a beta capacity cap on /v1/ask. Reads GET
          /v1/ask/usage on page load and after each answer -- see
          assets/js/page/faith-ask.js. Hidden until that first read
          succeeds, so a fetch failure degrades to "no meter shown"
          rather than a stuck "Loading…" line. --}}
    <div class="ask-usage" data-ask-usage hidden>
      <div class="ask-usage-row" data-ask-usage-mine hidden>
        <div class="ask-usage-meta">
          <span class="ask-usage-label">Your questions today</span>
          <span class="ask-usage-pct" data-ask-usage-mine-text></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ask-usage-bar"><span class="ask-usage-fill" data-ask-usage-mine-fill></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ask-usage-row">
        <div class="ask-usage-meta">
          <span class="ask-usage-label">Beta capacity today</span>
          <span class="ask-usage-pct" data-ask-usage-global-text></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ask-usage-bar"><span class="ask-usage-fill" data-ask-usage-global-fill></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="ask-status" data-ask-status role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

    <article class="ask-result" data-ask-result hidden>
      <h2 class="ask-question" data-ask-heading></h2>
      <div class="ask-answer" data-ask-answer></div>

      <p class="ask-gaps" data-ask-gaps hidden></p>
    </article>

    <p class="ask-error" data-ask-error role="alert" hidden></p>

  </div>

  {{!-- Citations rail. .ask-footnotes-wrap/.ask-footnotes are exactly
       the elements faith-ask.js already renders into
       (renderFootnotes() in assets/js/page/faith-ask.js).
       .ask-sources-empty is pure CSS: a sibling selector hides it once
       .ask-footnotes-wrap loses its `hidden` attribute, so the rail
       never sits blank before a first question is asked. --}}
  <aside class="ask-sources" aria-label="Sources for this answer">
    <div class="ask-sources-inner">
      <h3 class="ask-footnotes-title">Sources</h3>
      <div class="ask-footnotes-wrap" data-ask-footnotes-wrap hidden>
        <ol class="ask-footnotes" data-ask-footnotes></ol>
      </div>
      <p class="ask-sources-empty" data-ask-sources-empty>Ask a question. The pages it draws its answer from will list here, each one a link into the reader.</p>
    </div>
  </aside>
</div>
